Done several days ago, learned from the solution by @Vlad. Use 2D sweep with prefix sum to solve. For top-left (r1, c1) & bottom-right (r2, c2), just set

arr[r1][c1]++;
arr[r2+1][c1]--;
arr[r1][c2+1]--;            
arr[r2+1][c2+1]++;

Approach
Declare 2D vector arr(n+1, vector<int>(n, 0)) for less branches.
Proceed the loop, the step before scan:
for(auto& q: queries){
    const int r1=q[0], c1=q[1], r2=q[2], c2=q[3];
    arr[r1][c1]++;
    arr[r2+1][c1]--;
    if (c2+1<n){ // no need for c2+1==n
        arr[r1][c2+1]--;
        arr[r2+1][c2+1]++;
    }
}
Proceed sweeps columnwise by using prefix sum arr[i][j]+=arr[i][j-1] for i=0 to n-1 for j=1 to n-1.
Proceed sweeps rowwise by using prefix sum arr[i][j]+=arr[i-1][j] for j=0 to n-1 for i=1 to n-1
resize n for arr
Return arr
Worked out a variant with scan once without if-branches in the sanning.
